    "why": "The combination of fever, retro-orbital pain, myalgia/arthralgia, rash, and thrombocytopenia in an Aedes-endemic area (essentially all of urban and semi-urban India in season) is classic for dengue; serology/antigen testing confirms the diagnosis."
  },
  "redFlags": [],
  "investigations": [
    {
      "test": "NS1 antigen (early, days 1-5 of fever)",
      "why": "Detects dengue viral antigen early in illness before antibody response develops."
    },
    {
      "test": "Dengue IgM/IgG serology (later in illness)",
      "why": "Confirms diagnosis when NS1 is negative or testing occurs later in the course; IgG pattern can also suggest primary vs. secondary infection."
    },
    {
      "test": "CBC with platelet count (serial)",
      "why": "Tracks thrombocytopenia and hematocrit trends, central to monitoring for plasma leakage and bleeding risk."
    },
    {
      "test": "Hematocrit (serial)",
      "why": "A rising hematocrit with falling platelets signals plasma leakage and impending severe dengue — the single most important trend to follow."
    },
    {
      "test": "Liver function tests",
      "why": "Transaminitis is common in dengue and severe elevation can indicate more severe disease."
    }
  ],
